<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>IDEAS Research Institute, Warsaw, Poland
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>IDEAS NCBR, Warsaw, Poland
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we address the challenges associated with merging low-rank adaptations of large neural networks. With the rise of parameter-efficient adaptation techniques, such as Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), model fine-tuning has become more accessible. While fine-tuning models with LoRA is highly efficient, existing merging methods often sacrifice this efficiency by merging fully-sized weight matrices. We propose the <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Core Space</span> merging framework, which enables the merging of LoRA-adapted models within a common alignment basis, thereby preserving the efficiency of low-rank adaptation while substantially improving accuracy across tasks. We further provide a formal proof that projection into Core Space ensures no loss of information and provide a complexity analysis showing the efficiency gains. Extensive empirical results demonstrate that Core Space significantly improves existing merging techniques and achieves state-of-the-art results on both vision and language tasks while utilizing a fraction of the computational resources. Codebase is available at <a href="https://github.com/apanariello4/core-space-merging" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/apanariello4/core-space-merging</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, the size of neural networks has grown substantially <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, increasing the economic and computational costs associated with training from scratch and fine-tuning. As a consequence, efficient low-rank adaptation techniques have emerged, which enable broader access to these powerful models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>. Techniques like Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> reparameterize model updates to significantly reduce the number of trainable parameters. This makes it feasible for a broader range of users to fine-tune large architectures on their specific tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At the same time, the advent of model hubs such as Hugging Face <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> has simplified the diffusion of pre-trained and fine-tuned models, opening new opportunities for collaborative and multi-task learning by allowing users to acquire and build upon existing models easily. In this context, model merging, which aims to combine multiple specialized models into one capable of handling various tasks, has been gaining interest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. However, most prior works focus on fully fine-tuned models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. While this is practical for smaller architectures, fully fine-tuned versions of larger models are rare due to their high memory and compute costs. As model sizes grow, new strategies are needed to efficiently merge fine-tuned adaptations without incurring the prohibitive overhead of merging full models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, the authors observe that directly applying existing merging techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> to updates derived by multiplying low-rank components leads to suboptimal results. To address this, they introduce an alignment space that improves update compatibility. However, merging in this alignment space requires abandoning the low-rank representation and performing a singular value decomposition (SVD) on the horizontally concatenated full space updates. This suffers from two significant drawbacks: it eliminates the efficiency benefits of low-rank adaptation, and becomes prohibitively expensive as the base model size increases.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17786v1/teaser.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/216;" width="238" height="216"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Merging in full space is fast but suboptimal (bottom center). Merging in KnOTS space or using strong merging methods (<em id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text"></span>, TSV) improves performance but increases cost by orders of magnitude (right). <span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Core Space merging is effective and efficient (top left).</span> Results on Llama 3 8B.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome such limitations, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Core Space</span>, a novel parameter-efficient subspace that supports arbitrary merging techniques while retaining the benefits of low-rank adaptation. Core Space provides a common alignment basis for all task-specific low-rank components without loss of information. Notably, its dimensionality depends solely on the number of tasks and the LoRA rank, remaining tractable regardless of the base model size. Beyond its advantages in terms of efficiency, merging in Core Space also consistently improves the performance of existing merging strategies. We evaluate three setups: (1) first multiplying low-rank matrices and then applying merging techniques, (2) merging in the KnOTS space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, and (3) merging in our proposed Core Space. Across both vision and language domains, merging in Core Space achieves the best results – demonstrated on ViT-B/32, ViT-L/14, and Llama 3 8B backbones – highlighting that our approach not only preserves parameter efficiency but also leads to improved generalization and task performance (see <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this paper are the following:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Core Space Merging</span>, a framework to merge LoRA-adapted models in a shared low-rank basis, avoiding costly full space operations, while improving accuracy. Our approach can be easily integrated with existing merging methods.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that projection into Core Space ensures <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">no loss of information</span>, and provide a complexity analysis demonstrating the <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">efficiency gain</span> of merging in the proposed space.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present an extensive empirical evaluation showing <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">state-of-the-art results</span> achieved at a <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">fraction of the computational cost</span> of competing methods by merging in Core Space for vision and language tasks, including experiments on ViT-B/32, ViT-L/14, and Llama 3 8B.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT).</span>
Pre-trained models serve as a starting point for training experts specialized in various downstream tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. As the size of frontier models grows, the cost of fully fine-tuning such models increases accordingly. Therefore, several parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT), updating a small fraction of parameters, have been proposed, including adapters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, prefix tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and prompt tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Nowadays, LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and its variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, which rely on low-rank updates, have emerged as one of the most popular PEFT techniques.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model merging.</span> The abundance of available expert models inspired the fundamental question behind model merging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>: <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how can we integrate knowledge from multiple expert models into a single multi-task model?</span> Task Arithmetic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> proposed to construct <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">task vectors</span> (<em id="S2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span>, the parameter difference between expert and base model) and aggregate them via scaled addition, creating a multi-task expert. Since a significant performance gap between the single-task and the merged models remains, many approaches were proposed to address this issue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. TIES-Merging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> focuses on reducing sign conflicts between the parameters of expert models. Model Breadcrumbs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> removes outliers from the task vectors, while Consensus Merging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> eliminates catastrophic and selfish weights. Most recent methods, like TSV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and Iso-C <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, rely on singular value decomposition (SVD) of weight update matrices to reduce task interference when merging models. However, most methods are designed for merging fully fine-tuned models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Merging LoRA-adapted models.</span>
Methods designed to merge fully fine-tuned models do not necessarily transfer well to merging LoRA-adapted models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. The authors of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> proposed and improved a method to merge LoRAs. However, their approach relies on altering the fine-tuning procedure. KnOTS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> proposes to merge LoRA updates in the shared subspace, achieving a significant improvement. However, KnOTS performs SVD on the concatenation of full-size matrices instead of leveraging their decomposed update representations, making it costly, especially for large weight matrices. Therefore, finding a method that effectively and efficiently merges LoRA-adapted models remains an open challenge.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA fine-tuning.</span> Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> is a technique for efficient fine-tuning of large pre-trained models. Instead of updating the full model weights <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, LoRA introduces two learnable matrices <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\min(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\min(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>), and modifies the weight update as <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=W_{0}+\Delta W=W_{0}+BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=W_{0}+\Delta W=W_{0}+BA</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the original weight matrix. This significantly reduces the number of parameters that need to be updated during fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model merging. </span>
Given a set of <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> parameters <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{W_{1},\dots,W_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{W_{1},\dots,W_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, for a common architecture trained on <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> different tasks, a basic model merging approach calculates task vectors <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{i}=W_{i}-W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{i}=W_{i}-W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and computes a weighted sum <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{merged}}=W_{0}+\alpha\sum_{i=1}^{T}\Delta W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>merged</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{merged}}=W_{0}+\alpha\sum_{i=1}^{T}\Delta W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. When dealing with LoRA-adapted models we obtain a common <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and a set of decomposed, low-rank updates <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\Delta W_{i}=B_{i}A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Delta W_{i}=B_{i}A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, merging such weight matrices obtained from LoRA leads to suboptimal performance as shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, since LoRA-adapted models are less aligned w.r.t.<span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span> to their fully fine-tuned counterparts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>The Core Space Merging Framework</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Core Space Merging</span> (see <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), a framework designed to identify an effective and efficient subspace – referred to as the <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Core Space</span> – in which model merging for LoRA-adapted models can be performed while remaining in the low-rank regime. Core Space is designed to be reversible – it ensures no loss of information when projecting into Core Space and back to the original space – while being as compact as possible. Compactness allows for the use of state-of-the-art merging methods relying on Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) of weight matrices, which are highly costly to perform in the original space for large models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17786v1/method.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/209;" width="467" height="209"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full Space Merging</span> (left) firstly reconstructs full space matrices <math id="S4.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{(t)}=B^{(t)}A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{(t)}=B^{(t)}A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then performs merging in the full space to obtain <math id="S4.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S4.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KnOTS Merging</span> concatenates the <math id="S4.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, and performs a costly SVD on this high-dimensional matrix. Then, the <math id="S4.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices are merged and used to obtain the final <math id="S4.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>. The proposed <span id="S4.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Core Space Merging</span> (right) performs SVD on a concatenation of low-dimensional <math id="S4.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices to obtain reference bases. Afterwards, it performs SVD on the individual <math id="S4.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices and calculates the core matrices. It then performs merging in the Core Space and reconstructs to obtain the final <math id="S4.F2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Model Merging in Core Space</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the low-rank matrices for task <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, derived from a shared pre-trained base model <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each task update <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{(t)}=B^{(t)}A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{(t)}=B^{(t)}A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> can be reconstructed from the reduced SVD<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            We use the reduced SVD throughout, as it offers a suitable alternative for low-rank matrices where <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll m,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll m,n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> of the matrices:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}=U_{A}^{(t)}\Sigma_{A}^{(t)}V_{A}^{(t)\top}\!\!\!\!,\,\,\,B^{(t)}=U_{B}^{(t)}\Sigma_{B}^{(t)}V_{B}^{(t)\top}\!\!\!\!,\,\,\,\Delta W^{(t)}=U_{B}^{(t)}\Sigma_{B}^{(t)}V_{B}^{(t)\top}U_{A}^{(t)}\Sigma_{A}^{(t)}V_{A}^{(t)\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.677em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.677em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}=U_{A}^{(t)}\Sigma_{A}^{(t)}V_{A}^{(t)\top}\!\!\!\!,\,\,\,B^{(t)}=U_{B}^{(t)}\Sigma_{B}^{(t)}V_{B}^{(t)\top}\!\!\!\!,\,\,\,\Delta W^{(t)}=U_{B}^{(t)}\Sigma_{B}^{(t)}V_{B}^{(t)\top}U_{A}^{(t)}\Sigma_{A}^{(t)}V_{A}^{(t)\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The shapes of the matrices in the decomposition are: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{A}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{A}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{A}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{A}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{A}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{A}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{B}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{B}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{B}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{B}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Core Matrix)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the SVD of low-rank matrices <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">core matrix</em> <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{(t)}:=\Sigma_{B}^{(t)}V_{B}^{(t)\top}U_{A}^{(t)}\Sigma_{A}^{(t)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{(t)}:=\Sigma_{B}^{(t)}V_{B}^{(t)\top}U_{A}^{(t)}\Sigma_{A}^{(t)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, the core matrix <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact representation summarizing the directional transformation applied by the low-rank update of task <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, expressed in the singular spaces of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>. We confirm this intuition in <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Under the hypothesis that all tasks share approximately the same common bases <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{B},V_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{B},V_{A})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}\approx U_{B}^{(t)},V_{A}\approx V_{A}^{(t)},\,\forall t\in\{1,\dots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}\approx U_{B}^{(t)},V_{A}\approx V_{A}^{(t)},\,\forall t\in\{1,\dots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following holds:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Delta W^{(t)}\approx U_{B}\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T}M^{(t)}\right)V_{A}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Delta W^{(t)}\approx U_{B}\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T}M^{(t)}\right)V_{A}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">suggesting that, under aligned bases, the sum of low-rank updates (<em id="S4.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.SS1.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, Task Arithmetic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>) can be approximated by merging the corresponding core matrices – reducing the merging problem to operations in a much smaller <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times r</annotation></semantics></math> space. However, task-specific bases are misaligned in practice, making it impossible to directly compare or merge task-specific core matrices as in <a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Therefore, we aim to find a shared basis that can represent all tasks without loss of information. Intuitively, such a shared basis should span the subspace formed by the union of the individual task subspaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Reference Bases)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a collection of low-rank matrices <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A^{(t)},B^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A^{(t)},B^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define as <em id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reference bases</em> the orthonormal matrices <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}},V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}},V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by performing SVD over the vertically stacked <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and horizontally stacked <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> low-rank matrices across tasks.</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[B^{(1)};\cdots;B^{(T)}]=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}\Sigma_{B}V_{B}^{\top};\quad\begin{bmatrix}A^{(1)}\\
\vdots\\
A^{(T)}\end{bmatrix}=U_{A}\Sigma_{A}\left(V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}\right)^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[B^{(1)};\cdots;B^{(T)}]=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}\Sigma_{B}V_{B}^{\top};\quad\begin{bmatrix}A^{(1)}\\
\vdots\\
A^{(T)}\end{bmatrix}=U_{A}\Sigma_{A}\left(V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}\right)^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These bases span a shared latent subspace into which all task-specific updates are projected.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For each task <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we express the task-specific bases <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{B}^{(t)},V_{A}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{B}^{(t)},V_{A}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math> in the coordinate system defined by the reference bases <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}},V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}},V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math> by solving the following least-squares problems:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}R_{B}^{(t)}=\argmin_{R\in\mathbb{R}^{T\cdot r\times r}}\left\|U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}R-U_{B}^{(t)}\right\|_{F}^{2},\quad Q_{A}^{(t)}=\argmin_{Q\in\mathbb{R}^{T\cdot r\times r}}\left\|{V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}Q-{V_{A}^{(t)}}\right\|_{F}^{2},\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo>‖</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ref</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>Q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo>‖</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ref</mi></msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}R_{B}^{(t)}=\argmin_{R\in\mathbb{R}^{T\cdot r\times r}}\left\|U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}R-U_{B}^{(t)}\right\|_{F}^{2},\quad Q_{A}^{(t)}=\argmin_{Q\in\mathbb{R}^{T\cdot r\times r}}\left\|{V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}Q-{V_{A}^{(t)}}\right\|_{F}^{2},\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{A}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{A}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times T\cdot r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times T\cdot r}</annotation></semantics></math> (and similarly for <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>). These problems are convex, and since <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthonormal, setting the gradients to zero yields the global minimizers (see <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Least Squares Solutions ‣ Appendix A No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> for the full derivation):</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{B}^{(t)}={U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}U_{B}^{(t)},\quad Q^{(t)}_{A}={V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}V_{A}^{(t)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{B}^{(t)}={U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}U_{B}^{(t)},\quad Q^{(t)}_{A}={V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}V_{A}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The aligned core matrix <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> for task <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is then computed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}^{(t)}=R_{B}^{(t)}M^{(t)}{Q_{A}^{(t)}}^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>Q</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}^{(t)}=R_{B}^{(t)}M^{(t)}{Q_{A}^{(t)}}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we merge the set of aligned core matrices <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tilde{M}^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tilde{M}^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> using a function <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{merged}}=\mathcal{M}(\{\tilde{M}^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>merged</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{merged}}=\mathcal{M}(\{\tilde{M}^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes any existing merging strategy. We then reconstruct the final weight matrix from <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{merged}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>merged</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{merged}}</annotation></semantics></math> by projecting it back to the full space using the reference bases:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}M_{\text{merged}}{V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>merged</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}M_{\text{merged}}{V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Core Space merging offers several advantages over merging in the full space:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No loss of information</span> – transformation to core space and back to full space is lossless (see <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for the proof of zero alignment error). Moreover, when the merging function is linear (<em id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, Task Arithmetic), the model merged in full space will be the same as the model merged in core space <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and then reconstructed using the reference bases.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency</span> – aligned core matrices <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\cdot r\times T\cdot r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\cdot r\times T\cdot r}</annotation></semantics></math> have considerably lower dimensionality than full space matrices <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T,r\ll m,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T,r\ll m,n</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we can perform an expensive merging technique in the subspace of reduced dimensionality for a fraction of the total computational cost (see <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Computational Complexity Analysis ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> for detailed complexity analysis).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficacy</span> – in <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Results ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> we show that merging in Core Space yields better performance than merging in alternative spaces, when the merging function is <span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-linear</span>. In <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we shed light on the reasons for Core Space superiority. Our approach is found to yield compact representations of each task and improve alignment between subspaces of each task.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>No Information Loss in Core Space Representation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each task <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, reconstructing from the aligned core matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> exactly recovers the original low-rank update, <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{(t)}=U_{B}^{(t)}M^{(t)}{V_{A}^{(t)}}^{\top}={U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}}\tilde{M}(t){V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}=\left(U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}{R_{B}^{(t)}}\right)M^{(t)}\left(V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}{Q^{(t)}_{A}}\right)^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{(t)}=U_{B}^{(t)}M^{(t)}{V_{A}^{(t)}}^{\top}={U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}}\tilde{M}(t){V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}=\left(U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}{R_{B}^{(t)}}\right)M^{(t)}\left(V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}{Q^{(t)}_{A}}\right)^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The outer expressions are given by construction (see <a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eqs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#S4.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Definition 1 (Core Matrix). ‣ 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
The middle equality holds if <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{(t)}=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}{R_{B}^{(t)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{(t)}=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}{R_{B}^{(t)}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{A}^{(t)}=V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}{Q^{(t)}_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{A}^{(t)}=V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}{Q^{(t)}_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> or, equivalently, if the solutions of the least squares problems (defined in <a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) incur zero alignment error. Indeed, we show that the reference bases <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, computed via the SVD of the stacked matrices <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S4.E4" title="In Definition 2 (Reference Bases). ‣ 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), <span id="S4.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">minimize</span> the total alignment error across all <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> tasks, achieving an error of <span id="S4.SS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exactly zero</span>. To illustrate this, we first analyze the alignment error for a single task <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, explicitly focusing on <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Analogous conclusions hold symmetrically for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For clarity, in the following derivations, we assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\cdot r\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\cdot r\leq m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\cdot r\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\cdot r\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the total LoRA rank does not exceed the maximum possible rank of the target weight matrix. In <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Generalization Beyond the 𝑇⋅𝑟≤𝑚,𝑛 Assumption ‣ Appendix A No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>, we provide a more general formulation covering the case where this assumption is violated and show that the same zero alignment error result continues to hold.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemmax1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemmax1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma</span></span><span id="Thmlemmax1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemmax1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemmax1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemmax1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemmax1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemmax1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times T\cdot r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times T\cdot r}</annotation></semantics></math> be matrices with orthonormal columns, and let <math id="Thmlemmax1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{B}^{(t)}={U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}U_{B}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\cdot r\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{B}^{(t)}={U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}U_{B}^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\cdot r\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> be the optimal solution minimizing the error of the least-square problem. Then, the optimal alignment error is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{U}=\left\|U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}R_{B}^{(t)}-U_{B}^{(t)}\right\|_{F}^{2}=r-\left\|{U_{B}^{(t)}}^{\top}U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}\right\|_{F}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{U}=\left\|U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}R_{B}^{(t)}-U_{B}^{(t)}\right\|_{F}^{2}=r-\left\|{U_{B}^{(t)}}^{\top}U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}\right\|_{F}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The proof, provided in <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Alignment Error Quantification ‣ Appendix A No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>, leverages the properties of Frobenius norm and the orthonormality of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. To formally demonstrate that our chosen reference basis <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes the alignment error across all <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> tasks (or equivalently maximize <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||{U_{B}^{(t)}}^{\top}U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}||_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||{U_{B}^{(t)}}^{\top}U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}||_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for each task <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>), we first formulate the following constrained optimization problem for a single task, and then extend it to the multi-task scenario:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{U\in\mathcal{S}}\left\|{U_{B}^{(t)}}^{\top}U\right\|_{F}^{2}=\max_{U\in\mathcal{S}}\mathrm{tr}\left(U^{\top}{U_{B}^{(t)}}{U_{B}^{(t)}}^{\top}U\right),\quad\mathcal{S}=\left\{U\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times Tr}\;\middle|\;U^{\top}U=I_{T\cdot r}\right\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub><mo>‖</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>U</mi><mo>‖</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub><mi>tr</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>U</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>U</mi><mo>)</mo><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>U</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{U\in\mathcal{S}}\left\|{U_{B}^{(t)}}^{\top}U\right\|_{F}^{2}=\max_{U\in\mathcal{S}}\mathrm{tr}\left(U^{\top}{U_{B}^{(t)}}{U_{B}^{(t)}}^{\top}U\right),\quad\mathcal{S}=\left\{U\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times Tr}\;\middle|\;U^{\top}U=I_{T\cdot r}\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{tr}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{tr}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the trace operator. The optimization domain is restricted to the Stiefel manifold <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S4.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.SS2.p2.3.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, the set of matrices with orthonormal columns). The following lemma characterizes the solution to this optimization problem:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemmax2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemmax2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma</span></span><span id="Thmlemmax2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemmax2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemmax2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemmax2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A solution <math id="Thmlemmax2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to the quadratic program in <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2 No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> is given by a basis whose columns include the <math id="Thmlemmax2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvectors corresponding to nonzero eigenvalues of <math id="Thmlemmax2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}{B^{(t)}}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>B</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}{B^{(t)}}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> or, equivalently, by the <math id="Thmlemmax2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> left singular vectors of the matrix <math id="Thmlemmax2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, at the optimum, the objective attains its maximum value <math id="Thmlemmax2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in zero alignment error in <a href="#S4.E10" title="In Lemma. ‣ 4.2 No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We refer the reader to Appendix A.3 for a detailed proof. Briefly, the result follows by applying the method of Lagrange multipliers to augment the optimization objective with the Stiefel manifold constraint and then enforcing stationarity by setting the gradient of the Lagrangian to zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Extension to multiple tasks. </span> Achieving zero reconstruction error for a single model <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> does not guarantee optimality for any other model <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\neq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\neq t</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we aim to identify a reference basis <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that jointly optimizes <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2 No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> across all <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> models. We formulate this global problem as:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{U\in\;\mathcal{S}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathrm{tr}(U^{\top}{U_{B}^{(t)}}{U_{B}^{(t)}}^{\top}U)=\max_{U\in\;\mathcal{S}}\mathrm{tr}(U^{\top}\mathbf{U}_{B}\mathbf{U}_{B}^{\top}U)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{U\in\;\mathcal{S}}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathrm{tr}(U^{\top}{U_{B}^{(t)}}{U_{B}^{(t)}}^{\top}U)=\max_{U\in\;\mathcal{S}}\mathrm{tr}(U^{\top}\mathbf{U}_{B}\mathbf{U}_{B}^{\top}U),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{B}=\big[U_{B}^{(1)}\;\;U_{B}^{(2)}\;\;\dots\;\;U_{B}^{(T)}\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{B}=\big[U_{B}^{(1)}\;\;U_{B}^{(2)}\;\;\dots\;\;U_{B}^{(T)}\big]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the horizontal concatenation of all <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{B}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{B}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. The equality in <a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.2 No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> follows directly from the linearity of the trace operator and the distributivity of matrix multiplication concerning matrix addition: <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\top}A_{1}M+M^{\top}A_{2}M=M^{\top}(A_{1}+A_{2})M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\top}A_{1}M+M^{\top}A_{2}M=M^{\top}(A_{1}+A_{2})M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By considerations analogous to the single task-case, a global solution <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the top <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\cdot r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\cdot r</annotation></semantics></math> left singular vectors of the matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, obtained by vertically stacking each matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{*}=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{*}=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This choice ensures zero alignment error simultaneously across all <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> tasks, consistent with the procedure described in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exact merging via linear functions in Core Space. </span> Thanks to the lossless reconstruction of each task-specific update (see Eq. (<a href="#S4.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 4.2 No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>)), if the merging function <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linear</span> (such as in Task-Arithmetic), then merging in Core Space produces the same merged matrix as merging in full space. Specifically:</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(\{\Delta W^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T})=\mathcal{M}(\{{U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}}\tilde{M}(t){V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}\}_{t=1}^{T})=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}\mathcal{M}(\{\tilde{M}(t)\}_{t=1}^{T}){V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(\{\Delta W^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T})=\mathcal{M}(\{{U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}}\tilde{M}(t){V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}\}_{t=1}^{T})=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}\mathcal{M}(\{\tilde{M}(t)\}_{t=1}^{T}){V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">It is worth noting that Core Space merging is computationally efficient and does not increase time complexity – even when task arithmetic is applied in Core Space – due to its lower dimensionality, as we will show in the next section. Moreover, when the merging function is <span id="S4.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-linear</span>, we will show in <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Results ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> that it can improve performance compared to standard merging strategies applied in the full space at a fraction of the computational cost. For example, when dealing with Llama 3 8B, we have a speedup of more than <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="600\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>600</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">600\times</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to KnOTS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> for the Iso-C <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> method.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Core Matrix Alignment and Merging</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Low-rank updates <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(A^{(t)},B^{(t)})\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(A^{(t)},B^{(t)})\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, merging function <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Stack <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> vertically, <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> horizontally

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            Compute SVDs: <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{stack}(A^{(t)})=U_{A}\Sigma_{A}\underline{V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}\top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>stack</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>ref</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{stack}(A^{(t)})=U_{A}\Sigma_{A}\underline{V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}\top}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{stack}(B^{(t)})=\underline{U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}}\Sigma_{B}V_{B}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>stack</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{stack}(B^{(t)})=\underline{U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}}\Sigma_{B}V_{B}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> reference bases
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              Compute: <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}^{(t)}=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}B^{(t)}A^{(t)}V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>ref</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}^{(t)}=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}B^{(t)}A^{(t)}V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}\top}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            Merge aligned core matrices: <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathrm{merged}}=\mathcal{M}(\{\tilde{M}^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>merged</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathrm{merged}}=\mathcal{M}(\{\tilde{M}^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}M_{\mathrm{merged}}V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>merged</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>ref</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}}M_{\mathrm{merged}}V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}\top}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> reconstructed merged model
</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical Implementation. </span> While we introduce the per-task SVDs of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> for theoretical clarity, in practice the core matrix can be computed more directly as <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{M}^{(t)}\;=\;U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}^{\top}}B^{(t)}A^{(t)}V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>ref</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>ref</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{M}^{(t)}\;=\;U_{B}^{\mathrm{ref}^{\top}}B^{(t)}A^{(t)}V_{A}^{\mathrm{ref}},</annotation></semantics></math> without explicitly decomposing each LoRA matrix. This follows directly by substituting the definitions of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{B}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{B}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{A}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{A}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> into <a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, and by recalling the definitions of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eqs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Definition 1 (Core Matrix). ‣ 4.1 Model Merging in Core Space ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). This yields identical results and is marginally more computationally efficient. The decomposition of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> helps formalize the projection process and support the derivations in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, including orthogonality, rank preservation, and the zero-error reconstruction proofs. For this reason, we retain the SVD-based formulation in the theoretical exposition. We report in <a href="#alg1" title="In 4.2 No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> the algorithm for Core Space merging with this computationally optimized approach and the illustration in <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Optimized Pipeline ‣ Appendix D Additional Experiment Details ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Computational Complexity Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17786v1/time_complexity.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Core Space merging is more efficient than the previous state-of-the-art KnOTS.</span> The cost is similar to full space merging, which results in much lower performance. We visualize the number of operations performed to merge <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rank <math id="S4.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> LoRA modules of final shape <math id="S4.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> time complexities. The cheapest method is highlighted in <span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span> (<math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T,r\ll n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T,r\ll n</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:155.3pt;height:32.1pt;vertical-align:-14.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-41.3pt,8.5pt) scale(0.652894280793705,0.652894280793705) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Space</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">TA</td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Iso-C</td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">TSV</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.4.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Full</td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{n^{2}Tr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒏</mi><mn>𝟐</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{n^{2}Tr}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{3}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{3}T</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.4.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">KnOTS</td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{3}T^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{3}T^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{3}T^{2}+n^{2}Tr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{3}T^{2}+n^{2}Tr</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{3}T^{2}+T^{3}r^{2}n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{3}T^{2}+T^{3}r^{2}n</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.4.1.4" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;">
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T1.4.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;">[]<span id="S4.T1.4.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Core</span></span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" alttext="\bm{n^{2}Tr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝒏</mi><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝟐</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝑻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝒓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{n^{2}Tr}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" alttext="\bm{n^{2}Tr+T^{3}r^{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝒏</mi><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝟐</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝑻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝒓</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathbackground="#DFF1DF" mathvariant="bold">+</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝑻</mi><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝟑</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝒓</mi><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝟑</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{n^{2}Tr+T^{3}r^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" alttext="\bm{n^{2}Tr+T^{4}r^{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝒏</mi><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝟐</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝑻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝒓</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathbackground="#DFF1DF" mathvariant="bold">+</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝑻</mi><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝟒</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝒓</mi><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#DFF1DF;" mathbackground="#DFF1DF">𝟑</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{n^{2}Tr+T^{4}r^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the time complexities of TA, Iso-C, and TSV merged in all three spaces (Full, KnOTS, and our Core) in <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4.3 Computational Complexity Analysis ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tabs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Computational Complexity Analysis ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Details on the derivation of the complexities are in Appendix B.
Our approach exhibits a time complexity comparable to that of Task Arithmetic in full space. Our method’s additional terms are negligible unless the product <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\cdot r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\cdot r</annotation></semantics></math> becomes significantly large. A key advantage of our method lies in its scalability compared to KnOTS, whose time complexity is super-cubic, driven by a factor that scales cubically with the weight matrix size <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we emphasize the minimal additional overhead incurred when combining our method with Iso-C or TSV in the core space; it introduces a cost substantially lower than its counterpart in full space or KnOTS space.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental setup.</span>
We follow the experimental setup of KnOTS and use the LoRA checkpoints provided by the authors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>)</cite>. For the vision experiments, we use two variants of CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>)</cite> with ViT-B/32 and ViT-L/14 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> as vision encoders fine-tuned on a standard set of 8 tasks. We employ Llama 3 8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>)</cite> fine-tuned on 6 NLI tasks for the language experiments. All models are fine-tuned with LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>)</cite> with rank 16 applied on all matrices (keys, queries, values, and outputs) across all attention layers.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, we report <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">normalized accuracy</span> as a ratio of the accuracy of the merged model on a given task to the accuracy of the model fine-tuned on this task. We also report <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">absolute accuracy</span> for the joint-task setting (additional experimental details in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Additional Experiment Details ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline merging spaces.</span> We compare our proposed Core Space with two alternative merging spaces. <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full Space</span> operates in space of full reconstructed weight matrices <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W^{(t)}=B^{(t)}A^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W^{(t)}=B^{(t)}A^{(t)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KnOTS Space</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> operates in the space of the right singular vectors of the concatenated reconstructed weight matrices <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Delta W^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times nT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Delta W^{(t)}\}_{t=1}^{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times nT}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline merging methods.</span> We evaluate each merging space using the following merging methods. <span id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task Arithmetic (TA)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>)</cite> performs a scaled summation of each task matrix <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{merged}}=W_{0}+\alpha\sum_{i=1}^{T}\Delta W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>merged</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{merged}}=W_{0}+\alpha\sum_{i=1}^{T}\Delta W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. As this is a linear operation, the results of merging in each space are the same (see <a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4.2 No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ 4 The Core Space Merging Framework ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> for Core and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> for KnOTS). <span id="S5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TIES</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>)</cite> trims low-magnitude parameters and averages parameters with dominating sign, while <span id="S5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DARE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>)</cite> preprocesses task vectors by randomly dropping a fraction of parameters and rescaling the remaining ones. <span id="S5.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TSV</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>)</cite> concatenates low-rank approximations of task matrices and orthogonalizes them across tasks. <span id="S5.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CART</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> calculates centered task vectors as a difference of fine-tuned weights from the average of all fine-tuned weights and performs task arithmetic on the low-rank approximation of these centered task vectors. <span id="S5.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Iso-C</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>)</cite> flattens the spectrum of singular values for a model merged with task arithmetic. As the spectrum flattening can be performed on weights merged with any merging technique, we combine Iso with other merging techniques, denoting it with <span id="S5.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">+Iso-C</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Results</h3>

