<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Extension to VeRA</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also evaluated the applicability of Core Space merging beyond LoRA, specifically on VeRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> (<a href="#A5.T10" title="In E.3 Extension to VeRA ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). In VeRA, the decomposition <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=\Lambda_{b}B\Lambda_{d}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=\Lambda_{b}B\Lambda_{d}A</annotation></semantics></math> differs structurally from LoRA since <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed random matrices, and only the scaling vectors <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{b},\Lambda_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{b},\Lambda_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are trainable.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To apply our method, we absorb the scaling vectors into the low-rank matrices:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{B}=\Lambda_{b}B,\qquad\tilde{A}=\Lambda_{d}A," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{B}=\Lambda_{b}B,\qquad\tilde{A}=\Lambda_{d}A,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and then treat <math id="A5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{A},\tilde{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{A},\tilde{B})</annotation></semantics></math> as if they were standard LoRA components. Since the subsequent steps (stacking, SVD, projection, and alignment) are agnostic to how <math id="A5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> were obtained, the derivations in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A No Information Loss in Core Space Representation ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> apply without modification.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The empirical results in <a href="#A5.T10" title="In E.3 Extension to VeRA ‣ Appendix E Additional Results ‣ Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> confirm this reasoning: Core Space merging consistently outperforms other approaches even in the VeRA setting, validating that the framework is general to low-rank adaptation methods beyond LoRA.</p>
</div>
